Content: # Nine months after Hurricane Helene: Still mud and mess, but a powerful lesson in small-town resilience

\- Source:
—

Korey Hampton sees progress every day as she works along the French Broad River. But, nine months after Hurricane Helene, there are sti

**********Extracted claims**********

Korey Hampton observes daily progress while working by the French Broad River.
Nine months after Hurricane Helene, painful reminders and flashbacks persist.
Korey Hampton and her husband, Mitch Hampton, own French Broad Adventures.
French Broad Adventures is an outdoor activity company.
French Broad Adventures offers rafting, ziplining, and other activities.
French Broad Adventures operates in Western North Carolina.
Tourists are attracted to the area due to its scenic views.
The Appalachian Trail traverses the mountains in the area.
The French Broad River serves as a location for rafting and kayaking.
The Hamptons volunteer for the local raft rescue squad.
Floodwaters after the hurricane resulted in days of exhausting rescue operations.
Weeks after the hurricane, recovery of bodies took place.
Some businesses have reopened in downtown Marshall.
Other businesses in downtown Marshall are nearing reopening, as indicated by the sounds of construction.
Some buildings in the town remain closed.
Some buildings in the town are still filled with dirt and mud.
Debris piles persist along the river in difficult-to-access locations.
Some debris piles along the river contain logos of businesses located 20 miles or more upstream.
The water level rose 27 vertical feet during the flood.
Floodwater sometimes covered hundreds of horizontal feet.
Many trees are missing from the area.
The river appears wider.
Hurricane Helene made landfall as a Category 4 hurricane in Florida on September 26.
On September 27, churning conditions affected small towns located along rivers and streams beneath the eastern slopes of the Blue Ridge Mountains.
Cellular service was unavailable.
Radio communication was intermittent.
Local residents were unable to access online services or contact distant family and friends for days, or sometimes longer.
Misinformation proliferated on the internet in the period leading up to Election Day.
Misinformation was spread regarding the death toll.
Misinformation was spread regarding the federal and state response to the hurricane.
Hot Springs Mayor Abby Norton discussed Helene recovery efforts and challenges while walking the town’s Old Red Bridge.
Potter Josh Copus provided a tour of his ceramics studio.
Josh Copus uses several wood-burning kilns to fire, dry, and cure his ceramic work.
The Madison County Courthouse is located in downtown Marshall.
Big Pillow Brewery is located in Hot Springs, North Carolina.
Amy Rubin, owner of Big Pillow Brewery, spoke in the rebuilt brewing room.
Previously, President Biden was the focus of debate regarding post-hurricane response.
Currently, President Trump is the focus of questions concerning the speed and extent of federal assistance and the future of FEMA.
The 2020 census recorded Marshall's population as 846.
Hot Springs, located approximately 16 miles northwest of Marshall, had a population of 520 according to the census.
Madison County has a population of 22,000 people.
Madison County is situated northwest of Asheville.
Madison County is located where North Carolina meets Tennessee.
Trump and his allies contributed to the disinformation campaign following the floods.
Amy Rubin is a Democrat residing in Hot Springs.
The county where Amy Rubin resides voted 60 percent for Trump.
Amy Rubin and her husband own Big Pillow Brewing.
Big Pillow Brewing was one of the first businesses to reopen in Hot Springs.
Big Pillow Brewing owners are awaiting a check from their flood insurance.
Big Pillow Brewing owners proceeded with cleanup and rebuilding efforts despite not yet receiving their flood insurance check.
An unpainted board on the bar inside Big Pillow Brewing indicates the floodwater height within the building.
The outdoor music stage at Big Pillow Brewing is constructed from new lumber.
The building housing the sheriff's office, mayor's office, and other town offices remains in disarray.
Most of the mud has been removed from the basement of this building.
The hardware store is closed.
The inn where Korey Hampton performed a second-story rescue is closed.
The flood was primarily caused by debris transforming a bridge on Spring Creek into a dam.
Hot Springs Mayor Abby Norton took her first days off since September 27.
Mayoral elections in the area are nonpartisan.
Abby Norton is a Christian who states that prayer influences her major decisions.
Abby Norton stated that she voted third-party in November, following the flood, due to an inability to support either Trump or Harris.
Abby Norton states that processes, such as receiving a federal check for town office repairs, take too long.
Abby Norton is concerned by discussions from Trump and his team regarding the elimination of FEMA or significant reduction of the federal role in disaster relief.
Marshall is nearly 500 miles east of the Atlantic Ocean.
Marshall is more than 500 miles south of the coast along the Florida panhandle.
Madison County was not included on any hurricane watch list.
On September 27, Josh Copus was considering using sandbags and moving valuables to the second floor of his Old Marshall Jail Hotel.
Josh Copus was also considering moving or securing furniture on the restaurant patio located by the railroad tracks along the French Broad River.
At 9 o’clock, the water rose over the railroad tracks.
Water had not gone over the railroad tracks since 1977.
Within one hour, 4 feet of water accumulated in the town.
Josh Copus went to the highest step of the Madison County Courthouse.
The top step of the Madison County Courthouse offered a view of the river and Josh Copus's hotel.
After two hours, Josh Copus had to leave the courthouse as water was entering it.
At its peak, the floodwater surged 27 feet up the flood ruler on the side of the old jail.
Josh Copus had meticulously restored the old jail to serve as a hotel and gathering place.
There was no cell service.
The community began recovery efforts with shovels, dump trucks, and backhoes.
Neighbors contributed items to the recovery.
Buildings in the town were destroyed.
Josh Copus was still involved in shoveling efforts on Election Day.
A woman from the community claimed to have heard that FEMA condemned Marshall.
The woman stated she saw the information on the internet.
Individuals engaged in mud shoveling did not inquire about voting preferences or political affiliations of building or business owners.
Repairing new pipes, bridges, and the railroad crossing presents challenges for residents of Marshall and Hot Springs.
Residents of the area identify as Appalachian Americans.
Josh Copus is a potter.
Josh Copus is a gifted ceramics artist.
Josh Copus has a ceramics studio and kilns located in the woods outside Marshall.
Josh Copus organized musicians to record an album at the hotel during restoration work.
Proceeds from the album will support recovery efforts.

