Title: It’s not just humans – chimpanzees also like to follow trends, study shows
URL: https://edition.cnn.com/2025/07/10/science/chimpanzees-grass-behavior-scli-intl
Content: # It’s not just humans – chimpanzees also like to follow trends, study shows

—

Chimpanzees living in a sanctuary in Africa have developed a “fashion trend” for dangling blades of grass or sticks from their ear holes and their behinds, a new study shows.

**********Extracted claims**********

A new study indicates that chimpanzees follow trends.
Chimpanzees living in an African sanctuary developed a "fashion trend" of dangling blades of grass or sticks from their ear holes and their behinds.
Researchers at Chimfunshi Wildlife Orphanage Trust chimpanzee sanctuary in Zambia observed a female chimpanzee beginning to dangle objects from her ear in 2010.
The dangling behavior was subsequently copied by other members of the female chimpanzee's group.
Ed van Leeuwen is the lead author of the study.
Ed van Leeuwen is an assistant professor of behavioural biology at Utrecht University in the Netherlands.
No evidence indicated that chimpanzees used the grass or sticks to alleviate pain or itching.
Chimpanzees appeared very relaxed when performing the dangling behavior.
Ed van Leeuwen characterized the behavior as a "fashion trend or social tradition."
Chimpanzees in a different group at the sanctuary began demonstrating the same dangling behavior over a decade after the first group.
Some chimpanzees in the second group also inserted objects into their rectums.
The second group of chimpanzees lived approximately nine miles from the first group.
The second group of chimpanzees could not have copied the behavior directly from the first group due to their distance.
Staff members in one area of the reserve had developed a habit of cleaning their ears with matchsticks or twigs.
Staff members in another area of the reserve did not have this ear-cleaning habit.
Ed van Leeuwen hypothesizes that chimpanzees in the first area learned the behavior from caregivers.
Ed van Leeuwen hypothesizes that the behavior was then transmitted to other members of the chimpanzee group.
Caregivers influenced the behavior in the second group of chimpanzees.
The second chimpanzee group later developed the practice of inserting sticks and grass into their rectums.
Ed van Leeuwen stated that the trend spreads through social learning.
Ed van Leeuwen cited an example of a group of chimpanzees at a zoo in the Netherlands where one female chimpanzee started walking as if she were carrying a baby when she was not.
All female chimpanzees in that zoo group adopted the specific walking style.
A new female chimpanzee who quickly adopted the walking style was integrated rapidly into the group.
A new female chimpanzee who refused to adopt the walking style took longer to be accepted into the group.
Ed van Leeuwen suggests these behaviors are related to fitting in and smoothing social relationships among chimpanzees.
The grass dangling behavior was primarily observed during chimpanzees' leisure time.
Chimpanzees engaged in the dangling behavior when they congregated to groom and play.
Chimpanzees living in the sanctuary do not have to worry about predators.
Chimpanzees living in the sanctuary do not face competition from other groups.
Chimpanzees in the sanctuary have more leisure time compared to their wild counterparts.
Elodie Freymann is a post-doctoral affiliate at the University of Oxford’s Primate Models for Behavioural Evolution Lab.
Elodie Freymann was not involved in the study.
Elodie Freymann stated that these types of observations are key to advancing the understanding of the origins and transmission patterns of cultural behaviors in chimpanzees and other non-human animals.
Elodie Freymann described the potential for interspecies copying between chimpanzees and their human caretakers as "mind blowing."

**********Claims for hard query**********

Staff members in another area of the reserve did not have this ear-cleaning habit.
Ed van Leeuwen is an assistant professor of behavioural biology at Utrecht University in the Netherlands.
Elodie Freymann was not involved in the study.

**********Formulated hard query**********

Chimpanzees living in a sanctuary in Africa have developed a “fashion trend” in a certain way, a new study shows.
In 2010, researchers working in a certain place observed how a female chimp started to dangle objects from her ear, and the behavior was soon copied by other members of her group, study lead author Ed van Leeuwen, an assistant professor of behavioural biology at Utrecht University in the Netherlands, told CNN on Wednesday.
There was no evidence that the chimpanzees were using the grass or sticks to deal with pain or itches, and they were in a certain state when they did it, Van Leeuwen said.

**********Claims for medium query**********

The second group of chimpanzees could not have copied the behavior directly from the first group due to their distance.
The grass dangling behavior was primarily observed during chimpanzees' leisure time.
Some chimpanzees in the second group also inserted objects into their rectums.

