Title: Trump and his allies mount a pressure campaign against US elections ahead of the midterms
URL: https://edition.cnn.com/2025/08/02/politics/trump-elections-pressure-campaign
Content: # Trump and his allies mount a pressure campaign against US elections ahead of the midterms

A few weeks ago, Republican election officials in Colorado began receiving unsolicited calls and texts from a GOP consultant who said he was working with the Trum

**********Extracted claims**********

Republican election officials in Colorado received unsolicited calls and texts from a GOP consultant.
The GOP consultant stated he was working with the Trump administration on “election integrity.”
The consultant, Jeff Small, indicated in a text to an election official that he was acting on a request from Stephen Miller, the White House deputy chief of staff.
Jeff Small stated in a phone call with an election clerk that he was coordinating with the White House and the Justice Department to implement an elections executive order signed by President Donald Trump.
Justin Grantham, the top election official in Fremont County, recalled Jeff Small's statement about coordinating with the White House and Justice Department.
Justin Grantham and Carly Koppes told CNN that Jeff Small requested they give a third party access to their election equipment.
Carly Koppes oversees elections in Weld County in northern Colorado.
Justin Grantham and Carly Koppes declined Jeff Small's request.
Carly Koppes stated that access to her election equipment would be denied.
The outreach to the Colorado clerks is part of federal actions launched by the Trump administration and groups aligned with the president.
The White House distanced itself from Jeff Small.
Donald Trump and his allies are collecting voter data.
Donald Trump and his allies are working to change the ground rules for the upcoming midterms.
Donald Trump and his allies often invoke federal government authority.
Democrats need to net three seats in the US House in 2026 to flip control of the chamber from Republicans.
Samantha Tarazi, CEO of the nonprofit Voting Rights Lab, stated her belief that Donald Trump is preparing to use his office's power to interfere in the 2026 election.
Samantha Tarazi stated that an executive order, which she described as unconstitutional, has evolved into a federal mobilization to gain power over elections.
White House spokesperson Harrison Fields stated that Donald Trump is fighting for election integrity.
Harrison Fields stated that Donald Trump will continue fighting for election integrity despite Democratic objections.
Harrison Fields stated that Democratic objections reveal disdain for safeguards like verifying citizenship.
Harrison Fields stated that free and fair elections are the bedrock of the Constitutional Republic.
Harrison Fields stated confidence in securing a victory in the courtroom.
Restricting access to election machines and sensitive voting software has become more important to election officials.
Voting system breaches have occurred in states such as Colorado and Georgia.
Donald Trump's allies sought access to machines to find evidence to support the claim of widespread fraud in the 2020 election.
Election watchdogs and some Democratic election officials state that activity by Donald Trump and aligned groups has raised fears of a broader effort to reshape elections.
Donald Trump signed an executive order in March.
Donald Trump's executive order sought to force states to require proof of citizenship for voter registration.
The executive order also sought to force "enforcement action" against states accepting mail ballots after Election Day.
Federal judges have blocked parts of Donald Trump's executive order.
Federal judges noted that the power to regulate elections rests with states and Congress, not the president.
The Republican National Committee (RNC) is attempting to obtain voter registration records from states.
The Republican National Committee sent records requests to 48 states and Washington, DC, on the day Donald Trump signed the executive order.
These RNC requests sought information on how states maintain voter registration lists.
The Republican National Committee has sued New Jersey.
The RNC alleges that New Jersey officials failed to respond to its requests for voter data and documents related to voting machine audits.
New Jersey is the location of a gubernatorial race.
A spokesperson for New Jersey’s elections division declined to comment on the litigation.
The Justice Department asked more than a dozen states to provide voter lists.
The Justice Department asked states to explain procedures for removing potential ineligible voters from their rolls.
The Justice Department asked states to discuss entering into information-sharing agreements to help the agency root out election fraud.
Justice Department demands include seeking copies of voting rolls in Michigan.
Justice Department demands include a broad request in Colorado for election records dating back to 2020.
Republicans in Texas are undertaking a mid-decade redistricting.
This redistricting follows entreaties from Donald Trump.
A map released by Republican lawmakers in Texas aims to gain five additional Democratic seats for the GOP.
This map would give the Republican Party the advantage in 30 of Texas's 38 congressional districts.
Republican lawmakers control the Texas state legislature.
The Republican-controlled House approved the SAVE Act in April.
The SAVE Act mirrors parts of Donald Trump’s executive order.
The SAVE Act requires proof of citizenship to register to vote.
The SAVE Act would make it a crime for election officials to mistakenly register someone to vote who has not provided proof of citizenship.
Texas Gov. Greg Abbott cited a letter from Harmeet Dhillon to justify redistricting in his state.
Harmeet Dhillon is the head of the Justice Department’s civil rights division.
Harmeet Dhillon's letter challenged the legality of four existing congressional districts.
Harmeet Dhillon stated in a statement that clean voter rolls and basic election safeguards are requisites for free, fair, and transparent elections.
Harmeet Dhillon stated that the agency has a statutory mandate to enforce federal voting rights laws.
Harmeet Dhillon stated that ensuring the voting public’s confidence in election integrity is a top priority of the administration.
Donald Trump has been blunt about his partisan goals in Texas.
Donald Trump has suggested that other GOP-controlled states should pursue their own redistricting efforts.
The administration’s actions have unsettled some election officials.
Election officials have endured years of threats and harassment following the 2020 election.
Conspiracy theories about election fraud flourished after the 2020 election.
David Becker, executive director and founder of the Center for Election Innovation & Research, stated that election officials are "surfing on quicksand."
David Becker is a former DOJ voting rights attorney.
David Becker stated that election officials do not know what the executive order means or if it has any meaning.
David Becker stated that election officials do not know if they will be investigated for doing their jobs.
David Becker stated that election officials do not know if the power of the federal government will be weaponized against them.
David Becker stated that election officials do not know if the Department of Justice will sue them.
A survey of 858 local election officials was conducted by the Brennan Center for Justice at New York University’s Law School.
The survey was conducted by email between April 15 and May 17.
The survey has a margin of error of +/-3 percentage points.
59% of local election officials surveyed are concerned about political leaders interfering with their jobs.
46% of local election officials surveyed are concerned about politically motivated investigations of their work.
The Center used a list of 8,491 local election officials, created with help from the US Vote Foundation, to invite participation in the survey.
Republican election clerks in Colorado began receiving calls and texts from Jeff Small in early July.
This outreach was previously reported by The Washington Post and Colorado media outlets.
Jeff Small has worked for Colorado GOP Rep. Lauren Boebert.
Jeff Small worked for the US Interior Department during Donald Trump’s first term.
Jeff Small is currently a principal with a Denver public affairs firm.
County officials interviewed by CNN stated that Jeff Small told them he was reaching out specifically to Republican clerks in blue states.
Jeff Small told officials his outreach aimed to help advance Donald Trump’s executive order.
Justin Grantham, the election clerk in Fremont County, stated that Jeff Small’s outreach to only Republican officials was an early red flag.
Justin Grantham stated that a concern arose when Jeff Small mentioned gaining access to the county’s election equipment.
Justin Grantham stated he did not believe the president had the constitutional authority to write executive orders affecting elections.
Justin Grantham stated he would not allow access to his voting equipment until the Supreme Court found the president could issue such orders.
CNN contacted Jeff Small.
Suzanne Taheri, Jeff Small's attorney, responded to CNN’s inquiry.
Suzanne Taheri stated in a text message that Jeff Small’s outreach supported efforts by administration allies.
Suzanne Taheri stated these efforts aimed to encourage officials to participate in President Trump’s election security executive order.
Suzanne Taheri stated that Jeff Small undertook the activity on a volunteer basis during his free time while on paternity leave.
Neither Jeff Small nor Suzanne Taheri answered questions about who in the administration asked Jeff Small to contact the clerks.
The White House distanced itself from Jeff Small’s actions in a statement.
A White House spokesperson stated in an email to CNN that Jeff Small does not speak for the White House.
A White House spokesperson stated that Jeff Small was never authorized to do official business on behalf of the White House.
Stephen Miller did not respond to CNN requests for comment.
There is heightened sensitivity in Colorado around who can access election equipment.
This heightened sensitivity follows the prosecution of former Mesa County elections clerk, Tina Peters.
Tina Peters became a celebrity among pro-Trump activists.
Pro-Trump activists have advanced false claims that voting machines were rigged in the 2020 election.
Many state laws set strict security standards for voting machines.
These standards aim to prevent tampering with elections.
Colorado has specifically barred third parties from accessing election equipment.
A judge sentenced Tina Peters to nine years in prison.
Tina Peters was convicted on state charges for her role in a breach of her county’s election system.
The breach was part of an unsuccessful effort to find fraud.
Donald Trump and his administration have supported Tina Peters' cause.
The Justice Department stated earlier this year it was reviewing Tina Peters' case.
The review is part of a broad mandate from Donald Trump.
The mandate aims to counter prosecutions that the Justice Department stated were aimed at "inflicting political pain rather than pursuing actual justice."
Donald Trump called Tina Peters an “innocent Political Prisoner” in a social media post in May.
Donald Trump directed the Justice Department to take all necessary action to help secure Tina Peters' release.
Colorado Secretary of State Jena Griswold stated to CNN that her office provided voter data to the Justice Department.
This voter data is generally available to the public.
Jena Griswold stated she declined to comply with a request for 2020 election records.
Jena Griswold stated her refusal was because the federal government has no legal basis to seek these records.
Federal law requires the preservation of election data in federal races for 22 months.
Jena Griswold stated that Donald Trump’s actions demonstrate Donald Trump is using the power of the federal government to undermine American elections and voter confidence.
Tensions over election administration remain high in Colorado.
Donald Trump lost Colorado in all three of his White House bids.
Carly Koppes, the Republican clerk of Weld County, stated she faced many threats for defending the 2020 election results.
Carly Koppes stated she faced threats for her county’s use of Dominion Voting machines.
Carly Koppes began to vary her routes to and from work due to these threats.
Carly Koppes continues to vary her routes to and from work.
Crane, the head of the clerk’s association, stated that it took courage for county clerks to rebuff recent overtures.
Crane noted the persistence of suspicion and harassment.
Crane noted that an elections office in southern Colorado, housing Dominion machines, was firebombed.
No one was injured in the firebombing incident.
Crane stated that threats against election officials are very real.

**********Claims for hard query**********

Suzanne Taheri stated that Jeff Small undertook the activity on a volunteer basis during his free time while on paternity leave.
The survey was conducted by email between April 15 and May 17.
No one was injured in the firebombing incident.

