**********Extracted claims**********

Carlo Zanella, president of the Alto Adige Alpine Club, blames travel influencers for a recent social media trend.
The social media trend has attracted hundreds of thousands of tourists to the Dolomites.
Many tourists are traversing private land to capture photographs in the Dolomites.
Local farmers have installed turnstiles in response to the increased tourist influx.
Tourists must pay 5 euros (nearly $6) to access several “Instagrammable” spots through the turnstiles.
The Instagrammable spots include the Seceda and Drei Zinnen (Three Peaks) mountain ranges.
Photos depicting lines of up to 4,000 people per day have appeared on social media.
These images have attracted more people to the locations instead of deterring them.
Italian law mandates free access to natural parks, including the Alps and Dolomites.
The landowners who installed the turnstiles have not received official pushback from authorities.
Georg Rabanser is a former Italian national team snowboarder.
Georg Rabanser owns land in a meadow on Seceda.
Georg Rabanser and other landowners began charging tourists to cross their land to make a point.
Many people pass through the landowners' properties daily and leave trash.
The landowners' action of charging tourists was a cry for help.
The landowners expected communication from provincial authorities but received none.
The landowners have only read statements in newspapers regarding the issue.
The landowners have not received warning letters.
