The Santa Cristina Tourist Board has employed four park rangers.
The park rangers are tasked with ensuring tourists remain on trails, avoid crossing meadows, and refrain from flying drones.
Lukas Demetz, president of the Santa Cristina Tourist Board, states that conditions have improved significantly.
Lukas Demetz states that the litter problem is not as severe as people claim and has considerably decreased.
Parking lots have been established across the Aosta Valley to prevent people from driving up the mountainside.
Hikers are mandated to use a paid shuttle bus to reach Monte Rosa.
At the Pian del Re peat bog in Piedmont, only 150 cars are permitted to park in the closest parking area.
Some regions, including Lake Braies, now impose a 40-euro charge per car to access the area.
Arno Kompatscher, the governor of South Tyrol province, has urged the national government to implement restrictions.
South Tyrol province is part of the Dolomites range.
The requested restrictions aim to protect fragile alpine ecosystems.
The restrictions also intend to discourage local residents from leasing their chalets to tourists.
New ordinances have characterized the 2025 summer across Italy.
Wearing only a swimsuit or being bare-chested in some Italian towns can incur a fine.
Semi-nudity can lead to a 500-euro fine in the interest of decorum.
The Tuscan island of Elba has implemented summer ordinances that forbid shirtless or swimsuit-only walking outside beach areas for both men and women.
The Ligurian city of Diano Marina has implemented summer ordinances that forbid shirtless or swimsuit-only walking outside beach areas for both men and women.
If attire is considered vulgar or "indecent," authorities can issue fines starting at 25 euros.
Walking barefoot is prohibited in Livorno.
