Title: Social Security says it will no longer send paper checks. What you need to know
URL: https://edition.cnn.com/2025/08/04/business/social-security-paper-checks?utm_source=business_ribbon
Content: # Social Security says it will no longer send paper checks. What you need to know

,

The Trump administration will soon stop sending out paper checks for Social Security beneficiaries, as part of an effort to fully modernize federal benefits payments. Th

**********Extracted claims**********

The Trump administration intends to stop sending paper checks to Social Security beneficiaries.
This action is part of an effort to fully modernize federal benefits payments.
Approximately 0.8% of all Social Security beneficiaries, representing a little over half a million recipients, must choose an alternative method to receive their benefits.
The Social Security Administration announced in mid-July that it would no longer issue paper checks starting on September 30.
Beneficiaries who have been receiving paper checks must choose between having their payments delivered electronically by direct deposit or receiving payments on a pre-paid debit card known as Direct Express.
The change to electronic payments is part of a broader government-wide initiative to modernize payment systems and enhance service delivery.
The US Treasury Department stated at the end of May that all federal payments, including Social Security benefits, tax refunds, and vendor payments, would be made electronically starting on September 30.
The transition to all electronic federal payments aligns with President Donald Trump’s executive order.
Paper checks are increasingly a gateway for fraud.
The Treasury is committed to raising awareness of the growing fraud risks associated with paper checks.
The Treasury aims to provide Americans with knowledge and tools to fight financial fraud and make informed financial decisions.
Electronic payments are more cost-effective than issuing paper checks.
Issuing paper checks costs about 50 cents each.
Sending funds electronically costs less than 15 cents.
Some exceptions will be made to the policy of not sending paper checks.
Senator Elizabeth Warren stated that Social Security Commissioner Frank Bisignano committed to allowing some exceptions to the new policy.
Commissioner Bisignano's plan is to start with beneficiaries who have checking accounts.
Commissioner Bisignano committed that no one would be left behind.
Commissioner Bisignano committed that people who still need access to paper checks will retain that access.
A Social Security spokesperson stated that the Social Security Administration (SSA) is proactively contacting beneficiaries to inform them about the change and the process to enroll in direct deposit or receive Direct Express cards.
The Social Security Administration spokesperson stated that paper checks will continue to be issued where a beneficiary has no other means to receive payment.
The mandate for federal payments to be made electronically predates the Trump administration.
The electronic payment mandate has been a matter of law for years for most payments.
The law has allowed federal agencies to make exceptions to the electronic payment mandate.
The GoDirect site includes a section on how recipients can request a waiver for paper checks.
Waivers for paper checks are granted only in rare circumstances.
One circumstance for a waiver is if electronic payments would impose a hardship due to a mental impairment.
Another circumstance for a waiver is if the benefits recipient lives in a remote geographic location lacking the infrastructure to support electronic financial transactions.
A third circumstance for a waiver is if the recipient is age 90 or older.
People who want a waiver can contact the Waiver Line at the US Treasury Electronic Payment Solution at 1-855-290-1545 to request a waiver application.
Recipients may print out a specific form and mail it to the listed address to request a waiver.

