Title: NFL shaken as America’s most popular sport is affected by a uniquely American problem days before games begin
URL: https://edition.cnn.com/2025/07/30/sport/nfl-nyc-shooting-spt
Content: # NFL shaken as America’s most popular sport is affected by a uniquely American problem days before games begin

Late July is usually a time of great optimism and excitement for the NFL. Training camps are underway, preseason games are set to offer chance

**********Extracted claims**********

A shooting occurred at the New York City building that houses the NFL’s offices.
The shooting affected America's most popular sport.
The NFL has been affected by gun violence.
The issue of brain trauma from football's repeated violent hits is a sensitive matter for the NFL.
Brain trauma from football's repeated violent hits was scrutinized before preseason games began with the Hall of Fame game in Canton, Ohio, between the Detroit Lions and Los Angeles Chargers.
Shane Devon Tamura, a 27-year-old from Las Vegas, killed four people and himself in a midtown Manhattan office building on Park Avenue.
The shooting was the deadliest in New York City since 2000.
NFL Commissioner Roger Goodell stated in a memo that the league employee seriously injured in the incident is surrounded by family and members of the NFL community.
A virtual town hall was scheduled for NFL personnel.
John Miller of CNN reported that Shane Devon Tamura was put on psychiatric holds in 2022 and 2024.
Police described Shane Devon Tamura's mental health history as documented.
The rifle used by Shane Devon Tamura was assembled using a part purchased by an associate, which bypassed background checks required by Nevada law.
Papers found on Shane Devon Tamura's body indicate he had grievances with the NFL and its handling of chronic traumatic encephalopathy (CTE).
CTE is a degenerative brain disease.
CTE can be caused by repeated head trauma.
CTE is associated with memory loss, confusion, impulse control problems, aggression, depression, impaired judgment, and suicidal behavior.
Researchers are examining whether contact sports can increase athletes’ risk for CTE.
The NFL first publicly acknowledged a connection between CTE and football in 2016.
There is no proof that Shane Devon Tamura had CTE.
The only way to confirm whether an individual has CTE is to examine the brain after death.
Shane Devon Tamura apparently called for his brain to be studied.
New York City Police Commissioner Jessica Tisch stated that part of Tamura's note read: “Study my brain, I’m sorry.”
Shane Devon Tamura was a former high school football player.
Shane Devon Tamura shot himself in the chest.
Other football players who died by suicide have shot themselves in the chest to facilitate brain study after death.
A former teammate stated that Shane Devon Tamura suffered multiple concussions while playing at Golden Valley High School in the Los Angeles area.
An NYPD official stated that authorities determined Shane Devon Tamura had no previous connection to the NFL.
A spokesperson for the New York City Medical Examiner’s Office stated that Shane Devon Tamura’s brain will be examined for CTE.
It took until 2016 for the NFL to state that football and CTE were linked.
A federal judge approved a class-action lawsuit settlement between the NFL and thousands of former players in 2015.
The class-action lawsuit settlement has resulted in more than $1.5 billion in claims being paid out.
The NFL is working on developing safer protective equipment, including helmets, that reduce concussions.
The NFL reported a record-low number of concussions in the 2024-25 season.
The 2024-25 NFL season saw an increase in players self-reporting concussion symptoms.
Guardian Caps are a soft-shell padding that fits over traditional football helmets.
Guardian Caps are more commonly seen in practice but can be used in games.
The NFL instituted a more robust concussion protocol.
Players suspected of having a brain injury are examined by an independent third-party doctor on the sidelines.
Players must clear the concussion protocol to return to play.
The NFL announced a 17% reduction in concussions in the 2024-25 season, including all practices and games in the preseason and regular season.
The NFL implemented a new kickoff rule that drastically changed how players set up on kickoffs.
The new kickoff rule was an effort to slow down a play that resulted in high-speed collisions leading to concussions.
The NFL stated that the new kickoff rule effort worked.
Concussions on kickoffs were down 43% from the 2021-2023 seasons.
The NFL is funding research into better understanding CTE.
There is widespread agreement that more needs to be learned about brain injuries.
Bob Costas stated that the NFL has responded to calls for action regarding head injuries in recent years.
Bob Costas stated that the NFL has responded as best it possibly can in the last decade, unless football stops being played.
Bob Costas stated that football can only be made less dangerous, not completely safe.
Kansas City Chiefs head coach Andy Reid commented on the shooting at the league office, stating it was "a bad deal."

