**********Extracted claims**********

Freeman Dyson theorized that a shell made up of mirrors or solar panels completely surrounding a star would be the ultimate solution to the energy problems of an advanced civilization.
Freeman Dyson first explained the concept of a shell around a star in a 1960 paper.
Freeman Dyson stated in a 1960 paper that an intelligent species should be found occupying an artificial biosphere completely surrounding its parent star within a few thousand years of entering the stage of industrial development.
Freeman Dyson took the idea of a Dyson sphere from Olaf Stapledon’s 1937 novel “Star Maker.”
Freeman Dyson was a professor emeritus at the Institute of Advanced Study in Princeton, New Jersey.
The hypothetical megastructures became known as Dyson spheres.
Freeman Dyson later clarified that Dyson spheres would actually consist of a loose collection or swarm of objects traveling on independent orbits around a star.
Freeman Dyson noted in his paper that Dyson spheres would give off waste heat detectable as infrared radiation.
Freeman Dyson suggested that looking for waste heat as a byproduct would be a viable method for searching for extraterrestrial life.
Freeman Dyson added that infrared radiation by itself would not necessarily mean extraterrestrial intelligence.
Freeman Dyson stated that one of the strongest reasons for searching for such sources was that new types of natural astronomical objects might be discovered.
Scientists in the 1960s were largely receptive to the idea that a search for waste heat would be a good place to look for alien civilizations.
George Dyson, a technology writer and author, is one of Freeman Dyson's six children.
Freeman Dyson argued that technology is the only thing humanity has any chance of seeing in a search for intelligence.
In the 1960s, there was no way to actually search for Dyson spheres.
In recent times, many researchers have looked for Dyson spheres, including those at the SETI Institute and Fermi National Accelerator Laboratory (Fermilab).
A new study looked at 5 million stars in the Milky Way galaxy.
The new study suggests that seven candidates could potentially be hosting Dyson spheres.
The finding from the new study is attracting scrutiny and alternate theories.
The study was published on May 6 in the journal Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society.
The authors of the study specifically set out to search for Dyson spheres in the form of infrared heat near stars that could not be explained in any other way.
The research team looked at stars located within less than 1,000 light-years from Earth, using historical data from telescopes that pick up infrared signatures.
Lead study author Matías Suazo is a doctoral student in the department of physics and astronomy of Uppsala University in Sweden.
The study started with a sample of 5 million stars.
The researchers applied filters to get rid of as much data contamination as possible.
Seven sources glow in the infrared, and the reason for their glow is unknown.
There is no conclusive evidence that the seven stars have Dyson spheres around them.
Natural causes that could explain the infrared glow include an unlucky alignment with a galaxy in the background overlapping with the star, planetary collisions creating debris, or the stars being young and surrounded by disks of hot debris.
The data used by the researchers comes from two active space telescopes: NASA’s Wide-field Infrared Survey Explorer (WISE) and the European Space Agency’s Gaia.
The data also comes from The Two Micron All Sky Survey (2MASS).
2MASS was a collaboration between the University of Massachusetts and NASA’s Jet Propulsion Laboratory.
2MASS took place between 1997 and 2001.
The candidate stars are all red dwarfs.
Red dwarfs are the most common type of star in our galaxy.
Red dwarfs are dimmer and smaller than our sun.
It is unclear whether the candidate stars have planets around them, as they have not been observed by planet-spotting telescopes.
Many thousands of exoplanets identified so far orbit red dwarfs.
Planets orbiting red dwarfs have a higher chance of being habitable, according to NASA.
An earlier study published in March also found infrared anomalies among a sample dataset of 5 million stars in our galaxy, using data from the same sources as the new report.
Gabriella Contardo, a postdoctoral research fellow at the International School for Advanced Studies in Trieste, Italy, led the earlier study.
Contardo's study identified 53 candidates for anomalies that cannot be well explained.
More research is needed on the data from the studies.
NASA’s James Webb Space Telescope is powerful enough to observe the candidate stars directly.
Securing access to the James Webb Space Telescope might take time due to lengthy, competitive procedures.
Building hypothetical Dyson spheres would require technology and raw materials far beyond humanity’s grasp.
Freeman Dyson suggested that Jupiter would need to be dismantled for the raw materials to build a Dyson sphere.
The supercolossal scale of Dyson spheres probably means they are very rare.
The work provides the first strong evidence that there are not many Dyson Spheres in our galaxy.
Study coauthor Jason Wright is a professor of astronomy and astrophysics at Penn State University.
The candidates found by Matías Suazo are rare and interesting objects worthy of further study.
Freeman Dyson died in 2020.
Over a dozen ideas bear Freeman Dyson's name.
William Press, the Leslie Surginer Professor of Computer Science and Integrative Biology at the University of Texas at Austin, was not involved in the study.
Freeman Dyson showed that three competing quantum theories were actually the same theory as a young scientist.
Freeman Dyson applied his genius to areas of astronomy, cosmology, the extraterrestrial realm, and nuclear proliferation.
Freeman Dyson was recognized as a provocative and creative thinker at the time of his death.
George Dyson stated that his father contributed to five fields of mathematics and eleven fields of physics, as well as to theoretical biology, engineering, operations research, literature, and public affairs.
Many of Freeman Dyson's ideas were controversial.
One of Freeman Dyson's guiding principles was "It is better to be wrong than to be vague."
The approach of the researchers behind the new study could offer a more fruitful path in the search for extraterrestrial intelligence.
Tomotsugu Goto, an associate professor of astronomy at the National Tsing Hua University in Taiwan, was not involved with the study.
Contamination by circumstellar debris disks remains a concern in identifying Dyson sphere infrared signatures.
A May 23 paper published in response to Suazo's study suggests that at least three of the seven stars have been "misidentified" as Dyson spheres.
The May 23 paper suggests that the misidentified stars could instead be "hot DOGs" (hot dust-obscured galaxies).
The May 23 paper states that the remaining four candidates could probably be explained as hot DOGs as well.
The search for Dyson candidates straddles different fields, including basic sciences, philosophy, and religion.
Zaza Osmanov is an affiliate of SETI and associate dean of the School of Physics at the Free University of Tbilisi in Georgia.
Osmanov was not involved with the research.
The radiation fingerprint of the seven Dyson sphere candidates might be explained by natural phenomena.
Osmanov stated that the hypothesis of artificial origin for any radiation should be the last explanation after exhausting all possible natural explanations.
Future research is necessary to rule out natural explanations for the radiation.
George Dyson believes his father, if alive, would be highly skeptical that these observations represent a technological signature.
George Dyson stated that his father thought searching for such phenomena would lead to the discovery of new, non-technological astronomical phenomena.

