Title: 4,000-year-old teeth reveal the earliest use of this psychoactive substance
URL: https://edition.cnn.com/2025/07/31/science/thailand-betel-chewing-neolithic-archaeology-intl-hnk
Content: # 4,000-year-old teeth reveal the earliest use of this psychoactive substance

For the first time, archaeologists have used advanced scientific techniques on 4,000-year-old dental plaque to confirm traces of betel nut chewing in ancient Thai communities.


**********Extracted claims**********

Archaeologists confirmed traces of betel nut chewing in ancient Thai communities.
The confirmation was made using advanced scientific techniques on 4,000-year-old dental plaque.
Betel nuts are commonly chewed as "quids."
A "quid" consists of a mixture of slaked lime and ground betel nuts, wrapped in a betel leaf.
Betel nuts contain psychoactive compounds.
These psychoactive compounds can boost energy, alertness, euphoria, and relaxation.
Betel nut is a stimulant.
Betel nut chewing can leave red, brown, or black stains on teeth.
Betel nut is considered the world’s fourth most commonly used psychoactive substance.
Caffeine, alcohol, and nicotine are more commonly used psychoactive substances than betel nut.
Hundreds of millions of people globally use betel nut.
Betel nuts have been identified at archaeological sites through plant fragments or stained teeth.
Circumstantial evidence indicates betel nut use dates back at least 8,000 years.
An international team of researchers identified betel nut chewing in an individual without dental discoloration.
Advanced scientific techniques were used for this identification.
The study was published in *Frontiers in Environmental Archaeology*.
The study provides the earliest direct biochemical proof of betel nut consumption in Southeast Asia.
This proof predates previous evidence of betel nut consumption in Southeast Asia by at least 1,000 years.
Piyawit Moonkham is an archaeologist at Chiang Mai University in Thailand.
"Invisible" traces of betel nut chewing were discovered in molars.
The scientific method used is highly sensitive.
The scientific method used is minimally invasive.
The method requires only tiny samples of plaque.
Thanik Lertcharnrit is an associate professor at Silpakorn University in Bangkok, Thailand.
Thanik Lertcharnrit is an expert in Southeast Asian archaeology.
Few archaeologists use residue analysis to infer prehistoric life, tradition, or culture.
The paper is considered pioneering in archaeological research in mainland South Asia, particularly in Thailand.
The paper is considered state-of-the-art in archaeological research in mainland South Asia, particularly in Thailand.
Researchers collected ancient dental plaque, also known as calculus, from Nong Ratchawat.
Nong Ratchawat is a Neolithic burial site in central Thailand.
The research team removed five-milligram scrapes of plaque from 36 dental samples.
The samples were taken from six individuals.
The method employed is liquid chromatography-mass spectrometry (LC-MS).
LC-MS extracts, separates, and identifies chemical compounds.
LC-MS measures a molecule's weight relative to its electrical charge.
A control sample was required before testing the ancient samples.
Control samples included dried betel nut, red limestone paste, piper betel leaves, catechu bark, and tobacco.
The ingredients for control samples were ground with human saliva.
Modern control samples were tested to validate the method.
Trace plant alkaloids were detected in three samples from one individual.
The detected alkaloids included arecoline and arecaidine.
Arecoline and arecaidine are main psychoactive compounds in betel nut.
The individual from whom samples were taken is identified as "Burial 11".
"Burial 11" is estimated to be a woman around 25 years old.
The technique does not destroy original samples.
The technique leaves remains intact for future study.
LC-MS is used in pharmaceuticals, food safety, and environmental testing.
The use of LC-MS in archaeology has been limited.
Dr. Melandri Vlok is a bioarchaeologist and lecturer in anatomy and physiology at Charles Sturt University in Australia.
Much archaeological work with LC-MS focuses on detecting proteins in dental calculus for dietary information.
Using LC-MS to detect compounds trapped in dental plaque is innovative.
The method requires expensive machinery.
An Orbitrap is an advanced mass spectrometer.
An Orbitrap identifies molecules by measuring their mass-to-charge ratio.
Many researchers lack access to the necessary expensive machinery.
LC-MS is used more routinely by larger labs such as Harvard and Max Planck.
The paper has a Thai first-author.
The research team included members from eight institutions across three continents.
Chemical residue analysis was conducted at Washington State University.
Moonkham studied for his PhD at Washington State University.
The study's control samples are a novel aspect.
The control samples created a standard for testing.
Betel nuts have a long association with hospitality and religious rituals.
Recent research on betel nuts focuses on their classification as a carcinogen.
Recent research also focuses on the correlation between betel quid use and oral cancers.
Betel nut chewing has significant implications for human health.
Betel nut chewing affects millions of people in tropical Asia-Pacific.
The Thai government has strongly discouraged betel nut chewing since the 1940s.
Betel nut chewing remains popular in rural areas of Thailand.
Betel nut chewing is uncommon in Thai cities and among younger generations.
Betel nuts have a long history in Thailand.
Betel nuts hold societal importance in Thailand.
Moonkham's grandmother stated that chewing betel nut cleans teeth and aids relaxation.
Moonkham's grandmother shared betel quids with friends, family, or colleagues.
Betel nut chewing can create a social bond.
Researchers are investigating possible reasons for the absence of tooth stains in the examined individual.
Possible reasons for the absence of tooth stains include different chewing methods, cleaning habits, or decay over time.
The research team plans to analyze more individuals from the Nong Ratchawat site.
Approximately 150 additional individuals from Nong Ratchawat could be tested for signs of betel nut use.
The scientific technique can be applied to a wide range of plant and food residues.
Applying the technique could open new avenues for understanding ancient practices.

