Title: You can slow cognitive decline as you age, large study finds. Here’s how
URL: https://edition.cnn.com/2025/07/28/health/cognitive-decline-pointer-study-wellness
Content: # You can slow cognitive decline as you age, large study finds. Here’s how

Video 
\- Source:

At 62, Phyllis Jones felt trapped in darkness. She was traumatized by her mother’s recent death, ongoing pandemic stress and an increasingly toxic work environm

**********Extracted claims**********

The Protect Brain Health Through Lifestyle Intervention to Reduce Risk (US POINTER) study is the largest randomized clinical trial in the United States.
The US POINTER study was designed to examine whether lifestyle interventions can protect cognitive function in older adults.
Participants in the US POINTER study were cognitively healthy individuals between the ages of 60 and 79.
To be in the US POINTER study, participants had to be completely sedentary.
US POINTER study participants were at risk for dementia due to health issues such as prediabetes and borderline high blood pressure.
Laura Baker is the principal investigator of the US POINTER study.
Laura Baker is a professor of gerontology, geriatrics, and internal medicine at Wake Forest University School of Medicine in Winston-Salem, North Carolina.
Approximately one-half of the 2,111 US POINTER study participants attended 38 structured team meetings over two years.
Structured team meetings for the US POINTER study were held in local neighborhoods near Chicago, Houston, Winston-Salem, Sacramento, California, and Providence, Rhode Island.
During structured team sessions, a trained facilitator provided guidance on how to exercise and eat for the brain.
The importance of socialization, the use of brain-training games, and the basics of brain health were explained in structured team sessions.
Team leaders in the structured program held groups accountable for logging blood pressure and other vitals.
Physical and cognitive exams were conducted by a physician every six months for participants in the structured program.
The other half of the US POINTER study's participants attended six team meetings to learn about brain health.
The self-guided group of US POINTER study participants was encouraged to select lifestyle changes that best suited their schedules.
The self-guided group of US POINTER study participants received no goal-directed coaching.
Participants in the self-guided group of the US POINTER study also received physical and cognitive exams every six months.
The US POINTER study is a $50 million study.
The US POINTER study was funded by the Alzheimer’s Association.
The two-year results of the US POINTER study were presented at the 2025 Alzheimer’s Association International Conference in Toronto.
The two-year results of the US POINTER study were published in the journal JAMA.
Participants in the structured program of the US POINTER study appeared to delay normal cognitive aging by one to nearly two years more than the self-guided group.
The self-guided group in the US POINTER study also improved their cognitive scores over time.
US POINTER study participants wore fitness trackers that monitored their activity.
US POINTER study teams were introduced to the Mediterranean-DASH Intervention for Neurodegenerative Delay (MIND) diet.
The MIND diet combines the best of the Mediterranean diet with the salt restrictions of the DASH diet.
DASH stands for Dietary Approaches to Stop Hypertension.
Familiarization with vital signs was a pillar of the US POINTER study program.
US POINTER study participants were encouraged to monitor their blood sugar.
Brain training was provided to US POINTER study participants via memberships to a Web-based cognitive training app.
Becoming better at socializing was a key part of the US POINTER study program.
Researchers tasked US POINTER study teams with assignments to encourage socialization, such as speaking to strangers or going out with friends.
As the US POINTER study progressed, researcher check-ins were reduced.
The US POINTER study has a wealth of data that has yet to be analyzed.
The US POINTER study collected data on exercise, brain training engagement, MIND diet scores, team meeting attendance, sleep, blood biomarkers, and brain scans.
The US POINTER study has limitations, including the potential for a practice effect.
Both groups in the US POINTER study appeared to have improved their global cognition, which includes thinking, learning, and problem-solving.
The US POINTER study was not designed to provide immersive lifestyle interventions needed for people with early stages of Alzheimer’s disease.
Dr. Dean Ornish is a professor of medicine at the University of California, San Francisco.
A June 2024 clinical trial found that a strict vegan diet, daily exercise, structured stress reduction, and frequent socialization could often stop the decline or even improve cognition in individuals already experiencing early-stage Alzheimer’s disease.
The US POINTER randomized clinical trial showed that moderate lifestyle changes in diet, exercise, and socialization can improve cognition in individuals at risk for dementia.
Dr. Dean Ornish is the creator of the Ornish diet and lifestyle medicine program.
Dr. Dean Ornish is a coauthor of “Undo It!: How Simple Lifestyle Changes Can Reverse Most Chronic Diseases.”
More intensive multiple lifestyle changes often improve cognition in individuals already diagnosed with early-stage Alzheimer’s disease.
The US POINTER study indicated that more moderate lifestyle changes may be sufficient to help prevent cognitive decline.
Studies have found that lifestyle interventions like getting exercise, eating a healthy diet, keeping an active social life, and staying on top of other health issues can help delay cognitive aging.
Two years is not sufficient time to track brain changes over time.
Maria Carillo is a study coauthor of the US POINTER study.
Maria Carillo is the chief science officer of the Alzheimer’s Association.
An additional $40 million has been invested in a four-year follow-up to the US POINTER study.
Over 80% of the original US POINTER participants have joined the four-year follow-up study.

