Title: Roman-era ‘church’ in Spain may have been a synagogue
URL: https://edition.cnn.com/2025/07/30/science/synagogue-castulo-spain-scli-intl
Content: # Roman-era ‘church’ in Spain may have been a synagogue

Archaeologists working at a site in Spain say they have uncovered evidence of what may have been a synagogue used by a hitherto unknown Jewish community.

While excavating the site, previously belie

**********Extracted claims**********

Archaeologists have uncovered evidence of a structure in Spain.
The structure is believed by archaeologists to have potentially been a synagogue.
The synagogue may have been used by a previously unknown Jewish community.
The excavated site was previously believed to be a church.
The previous belief dated the church to the 4th century.
Experts found materials and architectural evidence during the excavation.
The materials and architectural evidence led experts to hypothesize that the building was a synagogue.
A study detailing these findings has been published.
Artifacts, including fragments of oil lamps and a piece of roof tile decorated with menorahs, were found during excavations in Cástulo.
Cástulo was a former Roman settlement in southern Spain.
No materials with a clear association with the Christian faith have been found at the excavated site in Cástulo.
Archaeologists have found evidence of Christian worship at another site in Cástulo.
Bautista Ceprián is a study author.
Bautista Ceprián is an archaeologist with the Cástulo Sefarad Primera Luz project.
The excavated building has a squarer shape compared to Christian churches.
Christian churches typically tend to be more rectangular.
Archaeologists found a hole in the building that could have been for supporting a large menorah.
The foundations of a central raised platform, or bimah, were found in the building.
A bimah is common in synagogues.
A bimah is not common in churches.
No tombs were discovered at the excavated building.
The building was constructed near an abandoned Roman temple.
The association of an abandoned Roman temple with paganism would have been feared by Christian residents.
The collected evidence suggests the existence of a previously unknown Jewish community in Cástulo.
The study authors argue this conclusion.
Bautista Ceprián stated that the reinterpretation of the building from a church to a possible synagogue was based on logical reasoning.
The logical reasoning was based on historical and archaeological data.
There is a lack of written records regarding a Jewish community in Cástulo.
The lack of written records introduces an element of doubt regarding the hypothesis.
The study authors acknowledged this doubt.
The community would have lived alongside Roman citizens in Cástulo.
The population is thought to have disappeared.
The population is not named in the anti-Jewish law enacted by Visigoth King Sisebut.
Visigoth King Sisebut ruled what is now Spain from 612 to 621.
Jewish communities in other nearby towns are specifically named in the anti-Jewish law.
One possible explanation for the population's disappearance is that Christian clergy feared the local population would convert to Judaism.
Close and friendly relations existed between Christians and Jews in the region at the time.
Christian leaders were becoming increasingly influential in the Roman Empire.
Concern over conversion drove Christian leaders to foment fear of and opposition to Jewish communities.
Episodes of pressure on Jewish citizens to convert to Christianity began around the end of the 4th century.
Jewish citizens who refused to convert were invited to leave their hometowns.
This type of incident could have plausibly occurred in Cástulo between the end of the 5th century and the beginning of the 7th century.

**********Claims for hard query**********

A bimah is not common in churches.
A study detailing these findings has been published.
The study authors argue this conclusion.

**********Formulated hard query**********

A bimah is in a certain state in churches.
Something detailing these findings has been published.
The study authors argue something.

**********Validation Result**********

subquestion 'A bimah is in a certain state in churches.' is NOT supported by the context.
<reject> The content states that a bimah "is common in synagogues but not in churches," directly contradicting the idea that a bimah is found in churches. </reject>

**********Claims for medium query**********

This type of incident could have plausibly occurred in Cástulo between the end of the 5th century and the beginning of the 7th century.
Experts found materials and architectural evidence during the excavation.
Concern over conversion drove Christian leaders to foment fear of and opposition to Jewish communities.

**********Formulated medium query**********

This type of incident could have plausibly occurred in a certain place between the end of the 5th century and the beginning of the 7th century.
Experts found something during the excavation.
Concern over conversion drove someone to foment fear of and opposition to Jewish communities.

**********Claims for easy query**********

The synagogue may have been used by a previously unknown Jewish community.
The collected evidence suggests the existence of a previously unknown Jewish community in Cástulo.
The structure is believed by archaeologists to have potentially been a synagogue.

**********Formulated easy query**********

The synagogue may have been used by a certain community.
Certain evidence suggests the existence of a previously unknown Jewish community in Cástulo.
The structure is believed by someone to have potentially been a synagogue.

