Title: ‘It’s just PR’: Skittles, Hershey and Nestle are removing artificial colors
URL: https://edition.cnn.com/2025/08/04/business/maha-rfk-food-artificial-colors
Content: # ‘It’s just PR’: Skittles, Hershey and Nestle are removing artificial colors

—

The Trump administration declared victory after Kraft Heinz, Skittles and General Mills made splashy announcements to remove artificial colors — even taking credit for Coca-

**********Extracted claims**********

The Trump administration declared victory after Kraft Heinz, Skittles, and General Mills announced the removal of artificial colors.
The Trump administration claimed credit for Coca-Cola’s plan to replace high-fructose corn syrup with US cane sugar in a new product version.
The White House stated that President Trump delivered on the MAHA (Make America Healthy Again) initiative.
The White House touted that changes by companies would address the chronic health crisis affecting Americans.
Nutritionists and public health researchers state that companies have only made performative changes.
Many of the changes made by companies were already in progress due to consumer demand for natural ingredients, according to nutritionists and public health researchers.
The Trump administration's funding cuts for health care, food stamps, research, and public health programs contradict its goal of making Americans healthier.
Dr. Barry Popkin, a professor of nutrition at the University of North Carolina, stated that changes made by companies are cosmetic with no health impact.
Dr. Barry Popkin stated that these changes allow MAHA proponents to claim a victory.
Health advocates credit Robert F. Kennedy Jr., the Health and Human Services Department secretary, and his MAHA movement for raising awareness about unhealthy foods, chronic disease, and major companies’ influence on the American food system.
Researchers state that Robert F. Kennedy Jr.’s focus on synthetic dyes overlooks the broader issue of the widespread availability of cheap, convenient foods high in salt, sugar, and fat.
Dr. Barry Popkin stated that ice cream remains ice cream and soda remains soda, even without artificial flavors.
A spokesperson for the HHS stated that Robert F. Kennedy Jr. is dismantling the status quo that contributed to a nationwide chronic disease epidemic.
A spokesperson for the HHS stated that Robert F. Kennedy Jr. is eliminating bureaucracy and restoring integrity to federal health programs.
A spokesperson for the HHS stated that the HHS is addressing the fundamental causes of chronic illness that prior administrations did not confront.
Ultraprocessed foods constitute up to 70% of the US food supply.
Ultraprocessed foods include many popular brands of chips, cookies, candy, ice cream, and pre-made meals.
Studies have frequently associated ultraprocessed foods with obesity, cancer, diabetes, heart disease, and other health disorders.
Robert F. Kennedy Jr. has described the easy availability of ultraprocessed foods as a crisis.
Robert F. Kennedy Jr. oversaw the White House’s Make America Healthy Again Commission report released in May.
The White House’s Make America Healthy Again Commission report identifies ultraprocessed foods as a significant factor in a national increase of chronic illnesses, especially among children.
The agency has not yet implemented any substantial measures to reduce ultraprocessed foods.
Synthetic dyes are derived from petroleum.
Synthetic dyes are frequently used to make food and beverages brightly colored and appealing to consumers, particularly children.
Synthetic dyes have potential negative effects on animal and human health.
Potential negative effects of synthetic dyes include a possible increased risk of cancer and neurobehavioral issues in some children.
The US Food and Drug Administration banned red dye No. 3 in food, beverages, and ingested drugs in January.
Robert F. Kennedy Jr. has been urging food companies to voluntarily remove all food dyes from their products.
Many companies have been phasing out synthetic dyes for several years due to pressure from consumers, health advocates, and bans or restrictions in states such as California, Virginia, and West Virginia.
Kraft Heinz and General Mills have already eliminated synthetic color additives from most of their products.
The Trump administration recently commended Kraft Heinz and General Mills for pledging to remove synthetic colors.
Dr. Marion Nestle, an emeritus professor of nutrition and food studies at New York University, stated that removing artificial colors is nutritionally meaningless for giving junk foods the appearance of health foods.
Approximately 64% of consumers actively seek out snacks perceived as “good for them,” a figure that has risen significantly in recent years, according to market research firm Circana.
Yogurt, cheeses, and foods and drinks containing protein have become widespread due to their perceived nutritional benefits.
Companies have made voluntary commitments to remove artificial dyes from their products on prior occasions.
Many companies have reversed their prior commitments regarding artificial dyes.
The Consumer Brands Association is a trade group that represents major food, beverage, and household product companies.
The Consumer Brands Association did not directly respond to criticism about accountability.
The Consumer Brands Association issued a statement in April claiming that the industry has always prioritized transparency and will continue to ensure consumers have necessary information for informed purchasing decisions.
The Consumer Brands Association stated that broadly defining foods as ultraprocessed might lead to "demonizing safe, shelf-ready foods" and limit consumers’ access to nutritious foods.
Critics assert that many of the Trump administration’s policies weaken government efforts to improve the food supply and Americans’ health, despite HHS's focus on food additives.
A Congressional Budget Office estimate predicts that Trump’s tax and spending cuts package will result in 10 million additional people lacking health insurance by 2034.
The Urban Institute states that over 22 million families will lose some or all of their Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program (SNAP) benefits.
The law eliminated funding for programs providing cooking classes and nutrition education to SNAP recipients.
The Agriculture Department terminated two pandemic-era programs that assisted schools and food banks in purchasing from local farmers.
Aviva Musicus stated that the federal government has reduced SNAP benefits for millions of Americans.
Aviva Musicus stated that the federal government has removed health insurance coverage from millions of Americans.
Aviva Musicus stated that the federal government has cut programs designed to help farmers supply local foods to schools.
The Trump administration reduced billions in research funding.
The Trump administration terminated thousands of employees at the National Institutes of Health, Centers for Disease Control, and other agencies.
The top nutrition researcher at NIH, who specialized in ultraprocessed foods, departed from the agency.
The top nutrition researcher cited censorship under Robert F. Kennedy Jr. as the reason for their departure.
The HHS previously denied claims of censorship under Robert F. Kennedy Jr.
Dr. Jerold Mande, an adjunct professor at the Harvard School of Public Health and a senior policy official in the Obama and Clinton administrations, stated that research cuts and layoffs might impede the enactment of stricter regulations or investigations into the food industry.
Dr. Jerold Mande stated that tens of thousands of government personnel have been lost, impacting the ability to conduct investigations.
Dr. Jerold Mande credits Robert F. Kennedy Jr. for making food-related chronic diseases, primarily obesity, a political priority.
The MAHA Commission plans to release its second report on its strategy for improving childhood chronic obesity in August.

