Title: OPEC+ makes another large oil output hike in market share push
URL: https://edition.cnn.com/2025/08/03/business/oil-prices-market-opec
Content: # OPEC+ makes another large oil output hike in market share push

OPEC+ agreed on Sunday to raise oil production by 547,000 barrels per day for September, the latest in a series of accelerated output hikes to regain market share, as concerns mount over po

**********Extracted claims**********

OPEC+ agreed to raise oil production by 547,000 barrels per day for September.
This increase is part of a series of accelerated output hikes by OPEC+.
OPEC+ is increasing output to regain market share.
Concerns exist regarding potential supply disruptions linked to Russia.
The latest production hike by OPEC+ represents a full and early reversal of its largest output cuts.
The United Arab Emirates (UAE) has a separate increase in oil output.
The combined increase in output by OPEC+ members and the UAE amounts to approximately 2.5 million barrels per day (bpd).
This combined increase represents about 2.4% of world oil demand.
Eight OPEC+ members held a brief virtual meeting.
The meeting occurred amidst increasing U.S. pressure on India to halt Russian oil purchases.
U.S. pressure on India is part of Washington’s efforts to bring Moscow to the negotiating table for a peace deal with Ukraine.
President Donald Trump stated a desire for a peace deal by August 8.
OPEC+ cited a healthy economy as a reason for its decision to increase output.
OPEC+ cited low stocks as a reason for its decision to increase output.
Oil prices have remained elevated despite OPEC+ raising output.
Brent crude LCOc1 closed near $70 a barrel on a Friday preceding the article's publication.
Brent crude LCOc1 was near $58 in April, identified by the article as a 2025 low.
Rising seasonal demand partly supports oil prices.
Amrita Sen, co-founder of Energy Aspects, stated that strong oil prices at around $70 give OPEC+ confidence about market fundamentals.
Amrita Sen also stated that the market structure indicates tight stocks.
The eight OPEC+ countries are scheduled to meet again on September 7.
These countries may consider reinstating output cuts totaling around 1.65 million bpd at the September 7 meeting.
Two OPEC+ sources reported this potential consideration.
A specific layer of output cuts, totalling around 1.65 million barrels per day, is currently in place until the end of a subsequent calendar year.
OPEC+ in full includes 10 non-OPEC oil producing countries.
Russia is a non-OPEC oil producing country included in OPEC+.
Kazakhstan is a non-OPEC oil producing country included in OPEC+.
OPEC+ pumps about half of the world’s oil.
OPEC+ had been curtailing production for several years.
OPEC+ curtailed production to support oil prices.
OPEC+ reversed its production course during the calendar year in which the article is set.
OPEC+ reversed its production course to regain market share.
Calls from President Trump for OPEC to ramp up production partly spurred OPEC+ to reverse its course.
The eight OPEC+ members began raising output in April of the calendar year in which the article is set.
An output hike of 138,000 bpd occurred in April of the calendar year in which the article is set.
Output hikes of 411,000 bpd occurred in May, June, and July of the calendar year in which the article is set.
An output hike of 548,000 bpd occurred in August of the calendar year in which the article is set.
An output hike of 547,000 bpd is set for September of the calendar year in which the article is set.
The hikes from May onwards were larger than initially planned.
Giovanni Staunovo of UBS stated that the market has absorbed the additional barrels well.
Giovanni Staunovo attributed the absorption to stockpiling activity in China.
Giovanni Staunovo of UBS stated that attention would shift to a Trump decision on Russia scheduled for an upcoming Friday.
A voluntary cut of about 1.65 million bpd exists from the eight OPEC+ members.
OPEC+ also has a 2-million-bpd cut across all its members.
The 2-million-bpd cut across all OPEC+ members expires at the end of 2026.
Jorge Leon of Rystad Energy stated that OPEC+ has passed the first test.
Jorge Leon stated that OPEC+ fully reversed its largest cut without crashing prices.
Jorge Leon stated that the next task for OPEC+ will be harder.
Jorge Leon defined the next task as deciding if and when to unwind the remaining 1.66 million barrels.
Jorge Leon stated this must be done while navigating geopolitical tension and preserving cohesion within OPEC+.

