Content: # Ghislaine Maxwell moved to federal prison camp in Texas

,

,

This 2013 photo shows Ghislaine Maxwell in New York City.

Jeffrey Epstein accomplice Ghislaine Maxwell, who had been serving a 20-year prison sentence in Florida, has been moved to a lower-

**********Extracted claims**********

Ghislaine Maxwell has been moved to a lower-security federal prison camp in Texas.
Ghislaine Maxwell had been serving a 20-year prison sentence in Florida.
Ghislaine Maxwell is in the custody of the Bureau of Prisons (BOP) at the Federal Prison Camp (FPC) Bryan in Bryan, Texas.
Donald Murphy, a BOP spokesperson, confirmed Ghislaine Maxwell's custody at FPC Bryan.
Ghislaine Maxwell was sentenced in 2022.
Ghislaine Maxwell was sentenced for carrying out a years-long scheme with Jeffrey Epstein to groom and sexually abuse underage girls.
Ghislaine Maxwell has continued to appeal her conviction.
Ghislaine Maxwell's appeals include one to the Supreme Court.
Ghislaine Maxwell met in private with Deputy Attorney General Todd Blanche at the US attorney’s office in Tallahassee one week prior to the move.
Details of Ghislaine Maxwell's meeting with Todd Blanche have not been made public.
Ghislaine Maxwell's lawyer stated that Ghislaine Maxwell "honestly answered every question that Mr. Blanche asked."
Donald Murphy declined to give an explanation for Ghislaine Maxwell's move.
The Justice Department has not responded to questions from CNN regarding Ghislaine Maxwell's move.
Ghislaine Maxwell's prosecution and Jeffrey Epstein's prosecution have been a focal point for those who believe powerful people cover for each other’s crimes.
The notion of a cover-up spiked after the Justice Department declined to make public additional evidence in the Epstein investigation or prosecution.
Ghislaine Maxwell has publicly attempted to work with the government in several respects.
Ghislaine Maxwell's meetings with Todd Blanche lasted two full days.
Ghislaine Maxwell has offered to testify before Congress if major conditions, including immunity and receiving questions beforehand, were met.
The conditions for Ghislaine Maxwell's testimony before Congress have been rejected.
A minimum-security prison camp, such as the one in Bryan, is the least-restrictive type of facility among federal prisons.
Minimum-security prison camps house inmates considered to be low-risk, non-violent, and unlikely to escape.
Prison camps have very little or no fencing containing the inmates.
Inmates inside prison camps are able to move relatively freely.
Other inmates in the camp for women include Jen Shah and Elizabeth Holmes.
For an inmate like Ghislaine Maxwell to be transferred into a minimum-security prison, a top official inside the Bureau of Prisons must conclude that the inmate's risk to public safety has lowered.
The determination regarding lowered risk to public safety is based on recommendations from prison staff or good behavior.
