Title: Giant shoes found near Hadrian’s Wall spark mystery around the soldiers of ancient Rome
URL: https://edition.cnn.com/2025/07/12/science/giant-shoes-ancient-roman-fort-magna
Content: # Giant shoes found near Hadrian’s Wall spark mystery around the soldiers of ancient Rome

—

An ancient Roman mystery is afoot in the rolling hills of northern Britain.

Archaeologists have unearthed a stash of unusually large shoes at the ruins of a fir

**********Extracted claims**********

Archaeologists have unearthed a stash of unusually large shoes at the ruins of a first-century military fort along Hadrian’s Wall.
Hadrian’s Wall is a 73-mile (117-kilometer) stone barrier.
Hadrian’s Wall famously shielded the Roman Empire’s northwestern perimeter from foreign invaders.
The discovery of shoes at Magna Fort is raising new questions about the lives and origins of the fort’s inhabitants.
Giant leather soles were found at Magna Fort in May.
Thirty-four pieces of footwear were found at Magna Fort.
The footwear found at Magna Fort includes work boots and baby-sized shoes.
The discoveries at Magna Fort are helping to paint a picture of the 4,000 men, women, and children who once lived in and around the English site.
Magna Fort is located just south of the Scottish border.
Eight of the shoes found at Magna Fort are over 11.8 inches (30 centimeters) in length.
Eight of the shoes found at Magna Fort are a US men’s size 13.5 or greater based on Nike’s size chart.
The large shoes found at Magna Fort are larger than average by today’s standard.
The average ancient shoe found at a neighboring Roman fort was closer to a US men’s size 8.
Rachel Frame, a senior archaeologist, concluded that the large shoes found belonged to people with very large feet.
Magna Fort was built around AD 85.
Multiple different Roman military troops and their families moved into Magna Fort every few years.
Inscriptions on Magna Fort’s walls and altars recount settlements of Hamian archers from what is now Syria.
Inscriptions on Magna Fort’s walls and altars recount settlements of Dalmatian mountain soldiers from Croatia and Serbia.
Inscriptions on Magna Fort’s walls and altars recount settlements of Batavians from the Netherlands.
Roman troops stationed at the fort often left shoes, clothing, and other belongings in surrounding trenches.
New occupants built larger structures on top of the existing fort.
New occupants packed rubble and clay between walls, trapping belongings left by previous tenants.
Dr. Elizabeth Greene is an associate professor of classics at the University of Western Ontario.
Dr. Elizabeth Greene has studied thousands of shoes collected from the Vindolanda Roman Fort.
Vindolanda Roman Fort has been excavated since the 1970s.
Vindolanda Roman Fort is among the most well-studied Roman forts along Hadrian’s Wall.
The recently discovered Magna shoes share some similarities with those in the Vindolanda Fort collection.
The soles of the shoes from both Magna Fort and Vindolanda Fort are made from thick layers of cowhide leather held together with iron hobnails.
Only a couple of the shoes discovered at Magna Fort have some of the upper portions still intact.
Vindolanda Fort shoe styles include closed military boots, open work boots, sneaker-like shoes reaching just below the ankle, and sandals with leather fasteners.
At Vindolanda, only 16 out of 3,704 collected shoes measured over 11.8 inches (30 centimeters).
Ancient Roman military manuals often described the ideal recruit as being 5 feet, 8 inches or 5 feet, 9 inches in height.
Soldiers stationed around Hadrian’s Wall came from all around the Roman Empire.
Soldiers stationed around Hadrian’s Wall brought a wide diversity of physical traits to their settlements.
Romans near Hadrian’s Wall generally cremated their dead.
Romans near Hadrian’s Wall used a headstone to mark graves.
Any bones remaining around the settlements are likely from enemy, illegal, or accidental burials.
Few bones found at the Magna site were too soft and crumbly to provide insight.
The 2,000-year-old leather found at both Vindolanda and Magna sites is preserved by anaerobic, or low-oxygen, conditions of the soil.
The 34 shoes found at the Magna Fort are in worse condition than those retrieved from Vindolanda decades ago.
Rachel Frame attributes the worse condition of the Magna shoes to the changing climate.
Climate change influences underground soil conditions.
Climate change introduces more oxygen into underground environments.
In oxygen-rich soil, microbes thrive.
Microbes in oxygen-rich soil contribute to decay.
Acidic pH levels erode natural materials like leather.

